WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:08.466
This covers two lectures from 10-799: denoising diffusion
models, and score-based models.

00:00:08.916 --> 00:00:18.948
They are presented separately, but the second lecture ends by
showing they are the same thing viewed from two directions —

00:00:20.298 --> 00:00:27.426
Generative modelling means learning a distribution p-theta of
x from data, so that two things work.

00:00:27.876 --> 00:00:36.588
Sampling: draw a new point and it should look real. Density
estimation: given a point, assign it a sensible probability.

00:00:37.038 --> 00:00:48.942
The families split into likelihood-based — autoregressive
models, VAEs, normalizing flows, energy-based models — and

00:00:49.392 --> 00:01:00.792
The recurring difficulty is the same for all of them: sampling
directly from a complicated p of x is hard, while sampling

00:01:01.242 --> 00:01:08.346
So every method is, in some sense, a scheme for turning easy
samples into hard ones.

00:01:09.696 --> 00:01:19.464
Autoregressive models generate one element at a time. Fine for
text; for a 4K image that is over eight million forward

00:01:19.914 --> 00:01:31.050
VAEs are famously blurry. If the encoder maps different inputs
into the same region of latent space, the decoder learns to

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:41.124
GANs are unstable and suffer mode collapse — the generator
finds one thing that fools the discriminator and stops

00:01:41.574 --> 00:01:48.726
Normalizing flows need every layer to be invertible, which
constrains the architecture badly.

00:01:49.176 --> 00:01:53.088
And energy-based models have an intractable partition
function.

00:01:53.538 --> 00:02:01.242
So: is there a way to sample all at once, train stably, and
avoid the partition function?

00:02:02.592 --> 00:02:06.672
The diffusion idea is almost embarrassingly simple.

00:02:07.122 --> 00:02:20.682
Define a forward process that adds a small amount of Gaussian
noise at each step. Run it long enough and any image becomes

00:02:21.132 --> 00:02:30.324
Then learn to reverse it. If you can undo one small noising
step, you can chain those reversals from pure noise back to a

00:02:30.774 --> 00:02:39.918
The forward process is a Markov chain: each step scales the
previous image slightly and adds noise with variance beta-t.

00:02:40.368 --> 00:02:48.480
The reverse process is Gaussian too, with a mean and variance
the network predicts. That is the entire model.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:54.054
Here is the property that makes diffusion trainable at all.

00:02:54.504 --> 00:03:07.992
Composing Gaussians gives a Gaussian, so the t-step marginal
has a closed form: x-t equals root alpha-bar-t times x-zero,

00:03:08.442 --> 00:03:12.330
Alpha-bar-t is just the running product of the alphas.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:22.788
So to train at timestep 500, you do not simulate 500 noising
steps. You draw one epsilon and compute x-t directly.

00:03:23.238 --> 00:03:27.870
That is why training is cheap even though sampling is
sequential.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:35.868
The reverse process is Gaussian too, with a mean and
covariance the network predicts.

00:03:36.318 --> 00:03:42.294
First simplification: fix the variance. Now only the mean is
learned.

00:03:42.744 --> 00:03:50.328
Second: rewrite that mean in terms of the noise, using the
same reparameterisation as before.

00:03:50.778 --> 00:03:59.466
The training target collapses to the squared error between the
true noise and the predicted noise — the line marked 'easier

00:03:59.916 --> 00:04:06.732
And sampling becomes: predict the noise, subtract a scaled
version of it, add a little back.

00:04:08.082 --> 00:04:15.450
We want to maximise the log-likelihood of the data, but it
involves an integral over all the intermediate states.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:29.076
The trick is the one from the VAE lecture: multiply and divide
by the forward process, recognise an expectation, and apply

00:04:29.526 --> 00:04:33.942
Expand the bound and it splits into three kinds of term.

00:04:34.392 --> 00:04:44.016
A prior matching term, which is constant here because both
ends of the chain are fixed Gaussians. A reconstruction term

00:04:44.466 --> 00:04:54.306
And in the middle, a KL divergence at every timestep between
the true reverse posterior and the model's. That is the part

00:04:55.656 --> 00:05:03.600
That objective is correct and unpleasant to work with. DDPM
makes three simplifications.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:10.722
First, fix the forward process — do not learn the betas. That
makes the prior term constant.

00:05:11.172 --> 00:05:16.452
Second, fix the variance of the reverse process, and only
learn the mean.

00:05:16.902 --> 00:05:32.574
Third, and this is the clever one: apply the
reparameterisation trick again. Because x-t is a known

00:05:33.024 --> 00:05:41.904
So the whole objective becomes: predict epsilon, and take the
squared error against the epsilon you actually used. One line.

00:05:43.254 --> 00:05:46.854
The algorithms fit on one slide each.

00:05:47.304 --> 00:06:01.440
To train: sample a real image, sample a random timestep,
sample a noise vector, form x-t in closed form, and take a

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:12.450
Notice what is absent — no chain simulation, no backprop
through T steps. Each gradient step touches exactly one

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:25.788
To sample: start from pure Gaussian noise, predict the noise,
subtract a scaled version of it, add a little fresh noise for

00:06:26.238 --> 00:06:37.398
The asymmetry matters: training is parallel over timesteps,
and only sampling is sequential. That is the opposite of an

00:06:38.748 --> 00:06:42.036
Trained that way, on faces, this is what comes out.

00:06:42.486 --> 00:06:53.742
In 2020 this was the result that made the field pay attention
— a likelihood-based model matching GAN sample quality without

00:06:55.092 --> 00:07:02.580
Before moving on, it is worth putting diffusion next to the
VAE, because the derivation was suspiciously similar.

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:10.422
A VAE has a learned encoder mapping data to a compressed
latent, and a learned decoder mapping it back.

00:07:10.872 --> 00:07:18.840
It is trained by maximising a lower bound on the likelihood —
the same Jensen's-inequality move we just used.

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:23.010
Hold that picture and look at what diffusion actually is.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:27.456
It is worth seeing the structural relationship.

00:07:27.906 --> 00:07:34.842
A diffusion model is a VAE whose encoder is not learned — it
is the fixed noising process.

00:07:35.292 --> 00:07:44.004
The latent is the same dimensionality as the data, rather than
a compressed bottleneck, and there are T of them rather than

00:07:44.454 --> 00:07:47.334
The decoder is the learned denoiser.

00:07:47.784 --> 00:07:56.280
That is why the derivation looked so familiar: it is a
hierarchical VAE with a particularly convenient choice of

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:05.934
The network has to map a noisy image to a noise prediction of
exactly the same shape, which points naturally at a U-Net.

00:08:06.384 --> 00:08:16.896
Downsampling builds coarse features, upsampling rebuilds fine
ones, and skip connections carry high-resolution detail across

00:08:17.346 --> 00:08:26.538
Convolution is a good inductive bias for images, and the
architecture makes matching input and output dimensions

00:08:26.988 --> 00:08:36.516
Alternatives exist now — transformers in particular — but
U-Net dominated diffusion architectures for the first several

00:08:37.866 --> 00:08:43.770
That is the diffusion story. The second lecture starts
somewhere apparently unrelated.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:48.540
It asks: what if we stop trying to compute the likelihood at
all?

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:56.154
Every likelihood-based method struggles with the same object:
the normalising constant.

00:08:56.604 --> 00:09:05.940
But ask what you actually need. If the goal is only to sample,
you could do gradient ascent in data space — walk uphill in

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:14.910
For that you do not need p of x. You need the gradient of log
p of x with respect to x. That is called the score function.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:31.464
And here is the payoff: the log-density is log of unnormalised
density minus log Z, and log Z does not depend on x. So its

00:09:31.914 --> 00:09:44.226
No intractable normaliser, no adversarial training, no
invertibility constraint, and no chain rule over dimensions —

00:09:45.576 --> 00:09:50.304
Concretely, the score is a vector field over the data space.

00:09:50.754 --> 00:10:00.450
At every point it gives the direction of steepest increase in
log-density, with magnitude proportional to how steep that

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:07.692
Near a mode, the arrows point inward and shrink. Far from the
data, they point back toward it.

00:10:08.142 --> 00:10:18.078
A network that outputs a vector for each input can represent
this directly — and unlike a density, it does not need to

00:10:19.428 --> 00:10:24.204
So the pipeline has three stages and no likelihood anywhere.

00:10:24.654 --> 00:10:29.118
From data samples, estimate the score field by score matching.

00:10:29.568 --> 00:10:34.848
Then run Langevin dynamics on that field to produce new
samples.

00:10:35.298 --> 00:10:43.602
That is the entire method — and notice there is no encoder, no
discriminator, and no normalising constant.

00:10:44.952 --> 00:10:52.632
The natural objective is the Fisher divergence: the expected
squared difference between the true score and the model's.

00:10:53.082 --> 00:10:57.162
But we do not know the true score. That is what we are trying
to learn.

00:10:57.612 --> 00:11:12.900
Expand the square. The first term is constant in theta. The
cross term contains the true score, and applying integration

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:21.054
So the ground-truth score disappears entirely. That is
Hyvärinen's score matching, and it is elegant.

00:11:21.504 --> 00:11:31.104
It is also impractical. The trace of a Jacobian costs one
backward pass per input dimension. For images that is

00:11:32.454 --> 00:11:35.334
The fix is to change the target slightly.

00:11:35.784 --> 00:11:42.432
Instead of the score of the data distribution, learn the score
of the data convolved with a known Gaussian.

00:11:42.882 --> 00:11:55.554
Because you chose that noise, its conditional score has a
closed form: it is x minus x-tilde, over sigma squared — a

00:11:56.004 --> 00:12:02.652
No Jacobian, no ground truth needed. The model just learns to
point back toward the clean data.

00:12:03.102 --> 00:12:14.334
And if that sounds familiar, it should. Predicting the
direction back to the clean sample is, up to a scale factor,

00:12:15.684 --> 00:12:20.700
With a trained score model, sampling is gradient ascent in
data space.

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:30.774
Start from something easy — a Gaussian. Then take a small step
along the score, and add a bit of fresh noise scaled by root

00:12:31.224 --> 00:12:40.968
The noise term is essential. Without it you would climb to the
nearest mode and stop, giving one point rather than a sample

00:12:41.418 --> 00:12:51.930
With it, the chain explores, and under mild conditions
converges to the true distribution as the step size shrinks

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:57.252
That is Langevin dynamics, and it is the whole sampler.

00:12:58.602 --> 00:13:03.834
Run that naively and the samples are bad. There are three
reasons.

00:13:04.284 --> 00:13:23.100
First, the manifold hypothesis. Real data lies on a low-
dimensional manifold inside a high-dimensional space. Off that

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:34.542
Second, the score estimate is inaccurate in low-density
regions — because you fit it by expectation over the data, and

00:13:34.992 --> 00:13:48.288
And that is precisely where Langevin has to start. Your
initial Gaussian sample is almost certainly in a low-density

00:13:48.738 --> 00:13:59.634
Third, even in high-density regions, the gradient cannot
always distinguish high density from higher density, so the

00:14:00.984 --> 00:14:06.120
There is a third failure, and it survives even where the data
is dense.

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:16.122
On the left, samples drawn i.i.d. from a two-mode
distribution. On the right, samples from Langevin dynamics on

00:14:16.572 --> 00:14:20.004
Both find both modes — but the proportions are wrong.

00:14:20.454 --> 00:14:28.254
The score is a gradient, and a gradient tells you which way is
up, not how much total mass sits under each peak.

00:14:29.604 --> 00:14:34.452
All three problems have the same root: parts of the space have
no data.

00:14:34.902 --> 00:14:48.390
So add Gaussian noise to the data. It spreads mass everywhere,
lifting the distribution off the manifold, filling the low-

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:54.192
But the amount matters, and the two failure modes pull in
opposite directions.

00:14:54.642 --> 00:15:03.522
Too little noise and the gaps remain — you have not fixed
anything. Too much and you have destroyed the distribution you

00:15:03.972 --> 00:15:09.972
The resolution is not to pick a value. It is to use many noise
levels at once.

00:15:11.322 --> 00:15:16.986
Adding noise addresses all three at once, and this figure is
the reason.

00:15:17.436 --> 00:15:23.724
The perturbed density has support everywhere, so there is no
manifold to fall off.

00:15:24.174 --> 00:15:29.886
The perturbed scores are well defined across the whole space,
not just near the data.

00:15:30.336 --> 00:15:38.544
And the estimated scores now match them — the word 'Accurate'
covers the whole panel, where before most of it read

00:15:39.894 --> 00:15:45.558
The resolution is not to pick one sigma but to use a whole
sequence of them.

00:15:46.008 --> 00:15:53.376
Start at a large noise level, where the density is smooth and
the score is reliable everywhere.

00:15:53.826 --> 00:15:58.578
Then step down, each time starting from where the previous
level finished.

00:15:59.028 --> 00:16:07.428
By the last level the noise is negligible and you are sampling
from something very close to the data distribution.

00:16:08.778 --> 00:16:11.226
Visually, this is what annealing does.

00:16:11.676 --> 00:16:17.868
At high noise the samples are shapeless but the chain moves
freely across the whole space.

00:16:18.318 --> 00:16:23.694
As the noise drops, structure appears and the chain settles
into a mode.

00:16:24.144 --> 00:16:31.008
The large-noise phase solves the initialisation problem; the
small-noise phase supplies the detail.

00:16:32.358 --> 00:16:37.326
Put the two side by side and the resemblance is not a
coincidence.

00:16:37.776 --> 00:16:44.544
Write both as x-t equals lambda-t times x-zero, plus sigma-t
times epsilon.

00:16:44.994 --> 00:16:55.602
For NCSN, lambda is one and sigma grows — the variance
explodes as noise is added, and the signal is never

00:16:56.052 --> 00:17:05.484
For DDPM, lambda is root alpha-t and sigma is root one-minus-
alpha-t, so the total variance is preserved.

00:17:05.934 --> 00:17:17.070
That is the only structural difference. And the two networks
are related by a constant: the optimal score equals minus the

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:25.608
DDPM predicts the noise; NCSN predicts the score. They are the
same function, differently scaled.

00:17:26.958 --> 00:17:30.822
Laid out side by side, the correspondence is exact.

00:17:31.272 --> 00:17:39.696
Same corruption, written with different lambda and sigma. Same
loss, up to which quantity the network outputs.

00:17:40.146 --> 00:17:49.818
The only genuine difference is the sampler — ancestral
sampling for DDPM, annealed Langevin for NCSN.

00:17:50.268 --> 00:17:53.532
And even that is a choice, not a consequence.

00:17:54.882 --> 00:18:01.986
Both DDPM and NCSN use a finite ladder of noise levels — a few
hundred at most.

00:18:02.436 --> 00:18:07.284
The obvious question is what happens as that number goes to
infinity.

00:18:07.734 --> 00:18:15.054
The ladder becomes a continuum, and the discrete chain becomes
a continuous-time stochastic process.

00:18:16.404 --> 00:18:24.516
Now push it. Both methods use a finite number of noise levels.
What happens if that number goes to infinity?

00:18:24.966 --> 00:18:34.974
The discrete chain becomes a continuous-time stochastic
process, described by a stochastic differential equation with

00:18:35.424 --> 00:18:44.280
Both DDPM and NCSN fall out as special cases of the same
equation, differing only in what f and g are.

00:18:44.730 --> 00:18:58.890
DDPM has drift minus one-half beta-t times x, which pulls
samples toward the origin — that is what preserves variance.

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:05.580
Hence the names: variance-preserving SDE and variance-
exploding SDE.

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:13.266
The last piece is a result from 1982, well before diffusion
models existed.

00:19:13.716 --> 00:19:28.596
Anderson showed that any such SDE has a reverse-time
counterpart, and wrote it down. The reverse equation is the

00:19:29.046 --> 00:19:36.582
Look at what that means. The reverse process is fully
determined except for one unknown — the score.

00:19:37.032 --> 00:19:44.376
Learn the score by score matching, plug it in, and you can
generate by running any numerical solver backwards.

00:19:44.826 --> 00:20:03.450
That reframes every sampler as a solver choice. DDPM ancestral
sampling, DDIM, Euler-Maruyama, Heun, higher-order methods —

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:06.480
To summarise the pair.

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:17.466
A diffusion model is a hierarchical VAE whose encoder you
designed rather than learned, and the closed-form forward

00:20:17.916 --> 00:20:26.388
Score-based models come at it from the other side: give up on
the density, learn its gradient, and the partition function

00:20:26.838 --> 00:20:46.374
And the two are the same model. The optimal score is the
optimal noise prediction divided by minus sigma-t. One

00:20:46.824 --> 00:20:48.480
Four things left open.

00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:59.658
Sampling is still sequential and slow. The SDE view is what
later enables fast solvers and distillation, but that comes in

00:21:00.108 --> 00:21:07.908
The reverse-time SDE assumes the exact score; you have an
estimate, and that error is not analysed here.

00:21:08.358 --> 00:21:17.742
The ELBO derivation needs the reverse conditionals to be
Gaussian, which only holds for small steps — which is why T is

00:21:18.192 --> 00:21:24.648
And the noise schedule is treated as given, even though it
matters a great deal in practice.

